---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * The privacy page.
 *
 * Written from what the site actually loads rather than from a template. The
 * external hosts were read out of the built HTML — `googletagmanager.com` and
 * the self-hosted Umami, and nothing else; the fonts are the five `.woff2`
 * files served from this domain, so there is no Google Fonts request to
 * disclose.
 *
 * It says plainly that there is no consent banner. That is a real decision
 * with real implications in some jurisdictions, and a privacy page that
 * quietly omits it is worse than one that states it. Nothing here is legal
 * advice and it deliberately claims no compliance with any particular regime.
 */
const UPDATED = "31 August 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this website collects, what the Flume application does not, and what BitTorrent itself exposes."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="head__title">What this site collects.</h1>
    <p class="lead head__lead">
      Short version: two analytics tools on this website, and an application
      that collects nothing unless you switch it on and cannot identify you if
      you do.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap prose-page">
    <section class="block">
      <h2>The website</h2>
      <p>
        Two analytics tools run on these pages, and they are the only things
        here that load from anywhere other than this domain.
      </p>

      <div class="tools">
        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Google Analytics 4</h3>
          <p>
            Property <span class="num">G-KNGWP21KK5</span>. It <strong
              >sets cookies</strong
            > and sends page views, along with the usual coarse details — approximate
            location from your IP address, browser, device type, and the page you
            came from — to Google, where Google's own{" "}
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener"
              >privacy policy</a
            > applies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Umami, self-hosted</h3>
          <p>
            Runs on a server we control. It is <strong>cookieless</strong> and records
            page views without building a profile that follows you between sites.
            Nothing it collects is shared with anyone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Both are there on purpose rather than by accident. Google Analytics is
        blocked by a large share of the people who read a page like this, so
        running a self-hosted tool alongside it is the only way to know how much
        the first one is missing. The gap between the two numbers is the
        measurement.
      </p>

      <p class="note">
        <strong>There is no cookie banner.</strong> Google Analytics sets cookies
        and you are not asked first. That is worth stating rather than burying: if
        you would rather not be counted, any content blocker stops it, and Firefox
        and Safari block it by default.
      </p>

      <h3>What is not here</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No forms, accounts, or newsletter.</strong> There is nothing on
          this site you can submit, so there is nothing for us to store.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No third-party fonts.</strong> The five font files are served from
          this domain, so no request goes to Google Fonts or any other host.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No advertising, and nothing sold or shared.</strong>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The site is hosted on Sevalla and served through Cloudflare, both of
        which keep ordinary server logs of requests. Downloads are served by
        GitHub, so following a download link is a request to GitHub and their
        policy covers it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>The application</h2>
      <p>
        Flume collects nothing by default. It asks once, during first run,
        whether it may send anonymous usage counts, and sends nothing at all
        unless you say yes. Declining is permanent — it does not ask again.
      </p>

      <div class="tools">
        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Diagnostics report</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Never sent anywhere.</strong> Builds a report you can paste into
            a bug report, shown on screen first so you copy it yourself. Flume has
            no way to transmit it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Usage counts</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Off unless you turn them on.</strong> Settings → Privacy → Send
            anonymous usage counts. Everything below describes what happens only after
            you do.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>What usage counts contain</h3>
      <p>
        At most once an hour, and once when you quit, five fields go to a
        collector we run:
      </p>

      <table class="fields">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="num">installId</span></th>
            <td>
              A random UUID made on your machine when you consented. Not derived
              from your hardware, network, or username — it has no relationship
              to you, so it cannot be linked back to you or matched against any
              other application.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="num">appVersion</span></th>
            <td>Which version of Flume, e.g. 1.0.0.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="num">os</span></th>
            <td>macOS, Windows, or Linux.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="num">arch</span></th>
            <td>x86_64 or aarch64.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="num">events</span></th>
            <td>
              Which things happened — launched, a torrent added, a torrent
              completed, a setting changed, an operation failed, and a handful
              more.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Two things keep that from identifying anyone. Counts are sent as{" "}
        <strong>ranges rather than exact numbers</strong>, because a range is
        what anyone would graph and "the install with 1,483 torrents" is one
        identifiable person. And events are timed to the hour, not the second.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is <strong>no free-text field anywhere in the format</strong>.
        Every value Flume can send comes from a fixed list, which is why{" "}
        <em>which</em> setting you changed is sent and what you changed it to is not.
      </p>

      <h3>What it never collects</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What you download.</strong> No torrent names, file names, info hashes,
          or magnet links.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who you download it from.</strong> No tracker addresses, peer addresses,
          or DHT nodes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where you put it.</strong> No file paths, folder names, or drive
          names.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who you are.</strong> No account, email, name, machine identifier,
          hardware serial, or MAC address.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The collector is a Cloudflare Worker writing to a small database, and
        its source is{" "}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume/tree/main/collector"
          rel="noopener">in the repository</a
        > — around 250 lines, short enough to read in full. It does not store your
        IP address or your user agent: the code never reads either, and the database
        has no column for them. Cloudflare sees your IP the way any web server does,
        but nothing we control writes it down.
      </p>

      <h3>Turning it off</h3>
      <p>
        Same switch, immediately, with no restart. The install ID and any queued
        events are deleted from your machine. What was already received cannot
        be tied back to you — the ID is gone from your side and was never linked
        to you in the first place — so there is nothing to request the deletion
        of.
      </p>

      <p class="note">
        <strong>One honest limitation, in the diagnostics report.</strong> It strips
        paths, addresses, URLs, and info hashes by pattern, but torrent names have
        no pattern — they are matched against your current library. A log line naming
        a torrent you already removed has nothing to match and can survive. That is
        exactly why the report is shown before it is copied: you are the only person
        who can recognise it. Read it before you paste it.
      </p>

      <h3>Everything Flume connects to</h3>
      <p>
        A torrent client cannot be silent on the network. This is the complete
        list: trackers listed in the torrents you add, peers (directly or
        through your SOCKS5 proxy), the DHT if you leave it enabled, your router
        if UPnP is on, and the usage collector only if you opted in.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no update checker, no crash reporter, no analytics SDK, and no
        font or asset fetching — the fonts are bundled, so the interface renders
        with no network at all. The full detail is on{" "}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume/wiki/Privacy"
          rel="noopener">the app's privacy page</a
        >, including every event and what it carries.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What BitTorrent exposes</h2>
      <p>
        This part is not about Flume, and no client can change it. It is worth
        saying anyway.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Peers can see your IP address.</strong> That is how the protocol
          works — peers connect to each other directly. Anyone in a swarm can record
          who else is in it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DHT announces that you are in a swarm.</strong> You can turn it
          off in Settings; magnet links stop working when you do.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            >Flume can route peer connections through a SOCKS5 proxy</strong
          >, which changes what peers see. It routes peer traffic and is not a
          general-purpose anonymity tool. If anonymity is your threat model, a
          torrent client is not the control you are looking for.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:adamgreenwell@gmail.com"
          >adamgreenwell@gmail.com</a
        >, or open an issue on{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume-site" rel="noopener"
          >the repository for this site</a
        >. If something on this page is wrong or out of date, that is a bug and
        it will be fixed.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        This page describes what happens. It is not legal advice and it does not
        claim compliance with any particular regulation.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1rem + 5vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .head__title {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--t-display);
  }

  .head__lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .updated {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  /* The measure goes on the sections, not on the `.wrap` element. Narrowing
     the wrap itself leaves its `margin-inline: auto` centring the result, which
     indented the whole body away from the heading above it. */
  .prose-page {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .block {
    max-width: 78ch;
  }

  .block h2 {
    padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: var(--t-title);
  }

  .block h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
  }

  .block p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .block ul {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.35rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .block li::marker {
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .block strong {
    color: var(--fg-0);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .block a {
    color: var(--acc);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .fields {
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
  }

  .fields th,
  .fields td {
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    color: var(--fg-1);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .fields th {
    width: 9.5rem;
    padding-right: 1.25rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .fields tr:last-child th,
  .fields tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .fields th,
    .fields td {
      display: block;
      width: auto;
      border-bottom: 0;
      padding-bottom: 0;
    }

    .fields td {
      padding-top: 0.3rem;
      padding-bottom: 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
  }

  .tools {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tool {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.3rem 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  .tool h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .tool p {
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem !important;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--warn) 35%, transparent);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--warn) 8%, transparent);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-1) !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .tools {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
